## Statement

Let $k$ be any field and $n\geq1$. Multiplication by $t_i$ and partial differentiation $\partial_i$ define a unital left $A_n(k)$-module structure on $k[t_1,\ldots,t_n]$. If $\operatorname{char}(k)=p>0$, this representation is not faithful: $p_i^p$ is a nonzero element of $A_n(k)$ that acts by zero.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A field $k$, an integer $n\geq1$, and the polynomial ring $k[t_1,\ldots,t_n]$.

[F1] [[def-polynomial-ring-on-a-family-of-indeterminates]]: polynomial elements are finite $k$-linear combinations of distinct monomials $t^\gamma$.

[F2] [[def-polynomial-weyl-algebra]]: $A_n(k)=T(E_n)/J_{n,1}$, where $E_n$ has basis $x_i,p_i$ and $J_{n,1}$ is generated by the same-type commutators and $p_ix_j-x_jp_i-\delta_{ij}1$.

[F3] [[thm-polynomial-weyl-algebra-normal-ordered-basis]]: the monomials $x^\alpha p^\beta$ form a basis of $A_n(k)$.

[F4] [[thm-universal-property-of-the-tensor-algebra]]: a linear assignment from the generators to a unital associative algebra extends to a unital algebra homomorphism from the tensor algebra.

[F5] [[thm-quotient-ring-universal-property]]: a ring homomorphism whose kernel contains a two-sided ideal factors uniquely through the quotient ring.

[F6] [[def-left-and-right-modules]]: a unital algebra homomorphism into endomorphisms defines a unital left module action by evaluation.

[F7] [[def-generated-and-principal-ideals]]: the ideal generated by a set is contained in every two-sided ideal containing that set.

## Verification

1.1 On a monomial $t^\gamma=t_1^{\gamma_1}\cdots t_n^{\gamma_n}$ define $X_i(t^\gamma)=t^{\gamma+e_i}$, and define $D_i(t^\gamma)=\gamma_i t^{\gamma-e_i}$ when $\gamma_i>0$ and $0$ when $\gamma_i=0$; extend both maps $k$-linearly using [F1]. [given, F1, construct]

2.1 The $X_i$ commute because multiplication in the polynomial ring commutes; for distinct $i,j$, the $D_i$ commute because applying the two exponent-lowering maps multiplies by $\gamma_i\gamma_j$ in either order, and $D_i$ commutes with itself. For each $i,j$, applying $D_iX_j-X_jD_i$ to $t^\gamma$ gives $\delta_{ij}t^\gamma$: when $i=j$ the coefficient difference is $(\gamma_i+1)-\gamma_i=1$, and when $i\ne j$ the two terms agree. Hence the generator assignments satisfy [F2]. [F1, F2, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 Pointwise addition and scalar multiplication and composition make $\operatorname{End}_k(k[t_1,\ldots,t_n])$ a unital associative $k$-algebra: composition is associative, the identity map is its unit, and $k$-bilinearity follows by evaluating on polynomials. Send each generator $x_i,p_i$ to $X_i,D_i$. By [F4] this linear assignment extends to a unital algebra map from $T(E_n)$. Step 2.1 shows that it kills every defining relator in [F2]. Its kernel is a two-sided ideal because it is an algebra homomorphism, so it contains their generated ideal by [F7]. By [F5] it factors through the quotient $A_n(k)$; write $\rho$ for this factor, which is $k$-linear because the quotient map is surjective. Evaluation $a\cdot f:=\rho(a)f$ gives a unital left module by [F6]. If $\operatorname{char}(k)=p>0$, then for every monomial, $D_i^p(t^\gamma)=0$: it vanishes when $\gamma_i<p$, and otherwise its coefficient is a product of $p$ consecutive integers, hence divisible by $p$. Thus $D_i^p=0$. [F2, F4, F5, F6, F7, step 2.1, algebra, construct]

4.1 By [F3], $p_i^p$ is a nonzero normal-ordered basis monomial in $A_n(k)$, while step 3.1 shows that the representation sends it to zero. Therefore the representation is nonfaithful in positive characteristic. [F3, step 3.1, algebra] ∎
